<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helmuth von Moltke the Elder</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1800">1800</a>–<a href="/years/1891">1891</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Helmuth von Moltke the Elder was a Prussian field marshal and long-serving chief of the Prussian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a>. He directed the victories over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1866 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1870–1871, using railways and staff planning to great effect. His successes made possible the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> and shaped its military tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/helmuth-von-moltke-the-elder.jpg" alt="Helmuth von Moltke the Elder" width="314" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Günther · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Helmuth_von_Moltke_the_Elder_(by_Carl_G%C3%BCnther)_-_BNF_Gallica_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Prussian General Staff, raised to pre-eminence under <b>Helmuth von Moltke the Elder</b>, institutionalised strategic planning as a professional discipline.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Helmuth von Moltke the Elder</b>, using railways, the telegraph and the breech-loading needle gun, Prussia defeated Denmark in 1864, Austria at the Battle of Königgrätz in 1866 and France at the Battle of Sedan in 1870.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Parchim, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.43,11.85&amp;year=1859" aria-label="Parchim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4814 327 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Helmuth von Moltke the Elder"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5264 597h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5264 597h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.43,11.85&amp;year=1859">What was here in 1859? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Eternal peace, however, is a dream, and not even a beautiful dream, for war is part of God's scheme of the world.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Johann Kaspar Bluntschli on the laws of war, 1880 &#183; tr. Edmund von Mach, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13056/pg13056-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Every law necessitates an authority to watch over it and to direct its execution, but there is no power which can enforce obedience to international agreements.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Johann Kaspar Bluntschli on the laws of war, 1880 &#183; tr. Edmund von Mach, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13056/pg13056-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Nobody, I think, can deny that the general softening of men's manners has been followed by a more humane way of waging war.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Johann Kaspar Bluntschli on the laws of war, 1880 &#183; tr. Edmund von Mach, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13056/pg13056-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
